/*!
    \page qtbridges-rust-index.html
    \title Qt Bridges - Rust
    \brief How to get started with Qt Bridges using Rust.

    \section1 Introduction

    This library enables building modern Qt Quick user interfaces with a Rust
    backend. It allows you to run
    \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qmlreference.html}{QML} code and use Rust types
    in QML. This combines a declarative UI with Rust-based business logic.

    Running QML code and starting any Qt-based application is generally done
    through the
    \l
    {https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/struct.QApp.html}{QApp} type.

    Rust types that should be used in QML must be annotated with either a
    \l {https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/attr.qobject.html}{qobject} or
    \l
    {https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/attr.qobject_impl.html}{qobject_impl}
    attribute macro. Within those blocks you can use the
    \l{https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/macro.qproperty.html}{qproperty},
    \l
    {https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/attr.qslot.html}{qslot},
    and \l
    {https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/attr.qsignal.html}{qsignal}
    attribute macros to define how the Rust types appear in QML.

    The library provides special traits that enable Rust types to fulfill
    specific roles, including \l
    {https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/trait.QListModel.html}{QListModel}
    and \l {https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/trait.QTableModel.html}{QTableModel}.

    \section1 API example

    Exposing a Rust type to QML and starting the declarative UI is as simple as:

    Main.rs
    \code [rust]
    use qtbridge::{qobject_impl, QApp};

    #[derive(Default)]
    pub struct Backend {
    }

    #[qobject_impl(Singleton)]
    impl Backend {
        #[qslot]
        fn say_hello(&self) {
            println!("Hello World!")
        }
    }

    fn main() {
        QApp::new()
            .register::<Backend>()
            .load_qml(include_bytes!("qml/Main.qml"))
            .run();
    }
    \endcode

    The UI can then be described in a declarative way in QML:

    Main.qml
    \code [qml]
    import QtQuick
    import QtQuick.Controls
    import hello_world

    ApplicationWindow {

        visible: true
        title: qsTr("Minimal QML app")

        Button {
            anchors.centerIn: parent
            text: "Hello World!"
            onClicked: Backend.sayHello()
        }
    }
    \endcode

    \section1 Getting started

    \section2 Prerequisites

    To use this library, you’ll need:

    \list
        \li Linux (x86_64)
        \li Windows (x64)
        \li macOS (arm64) - experimental
    \endlist

    \list
        \li \b Rust (stable, version >= 1.87) - Visit
        \l {https://rustup.rs}{rustup.rs} to install
        \li \b {Cargo and rustfmt} (comes with Rust)
        \li \l {Qt installation}{\b {Qt 6 installation}}
        \li \b {C++ toolchain} (compiler, linker etc)
    \endlist

    Optional:
    \list
        \li \b {clang-format} available in build environment
    \endlist

    \section2 Source code

    \l {https://github.com/qt/qtbridge-rust}{Qt Bridge Rust repository}

    \section2 Qt installation

    The Qt installation must be present in the system and \c qmake must be in
    the system PATH. There are no special requirements to the Qt installation.
    It can be built from source or downloaded from \l {https://download.qt.io/}
    {download.qt.io}. To ensure \c qmake is available, add the Qt bin directory
    to your PATH, for example:

    On Linux:
    \code [shell]
    export PATH=<PATH_TO_QT>/bin:$PATH
    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="/home/john_doe/dev/qt_build/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH"
    \endcode

    On Windows:
    \code {shell}
    $env:Path = "<PATH_TO_QT>\bin;" + $env:Path
    \endcode

    On macOS:
    \code [shell]
    export PATH=<PATH_TO_QT>/bin:$PATH
    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="/home/john_doe/dev/qt_build/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH"
    \endcode

    \section2 Dependency

    QtBridge has a single crate with all public APIs:
    \code [toml]
    [dependencies]
    qtbridge
    \endcode

    \section2 Build using cargo

    \badcode
        cargo build
    \endcode

    \section2 Run using cargo

    \badcode
        cargo run --bin <binary_name>
    \endcode

    \section2 Run tests

    \badcode
        cargo test
    \endcode

    \section2 Examples

    Note: In the preliminary version of this repository, the package dependency
    is given as a relative path. With the final release, the package dependency
    can be resolved with cargo and crates.io. Until then you need to adapt the
    dependency or check out the whole repository.

    To run the applications, use \c {cargo run} from one of its
    directories.

