    \section1 API reference documentation

    Refer to \l
    {https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/index.html}{Qt Bridge
    Rust Crate API documentation} for the official API documentation.

    \section1 API overview

    \section2 Borrow checker and rules

    QtBridge aims to respect the borrowing rules of Rust. All QObjects in Rust
    (implemented with
    \l{https://doc-snapshots.qt.io/qtbridge-rust/qtbridge/attr.qobject_impl.html}{qobject_impl}) are designed to be held in
    \c {Rc<RefCell<_>>} structs. The QML engine also holds Rust objects through
    such references. With this construct, many references can be held and
    borrowed at runtime. Their QML representation can be copied following the
    ordinary JavaScript rules. A Rust object is only borrowed when a slot or
    property defined in Rust is invoked. If QML is unable to borrow the Rust
    object, it will panic. We are currently investigating alternative ways to
    handle this situation more gracefully.


    \section1 Further information:

    Qt itself is written in C++, and QtBridge builds on
    \l {https://cxx.rs/}{CXX} to access the required Qt interfaces. As a user,
    you do not need to write any C++ code. Instead, Rust structs and data can be
    exposed to QML using attribute macros provided by the library.

    If your project requires mixing Rust and C++ code, using Qt Widgets, or
    accessing Qt modules that only provide a C++ API, consider using
    \l {https://github.com/KDAB/cxx-qt}{CXX-Qt} instead.

    Internally, the library relies on Qt concepts, such as
    \list
        \li \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qobject.html}{QObjects},
        \li \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/properties.html}{properties}
        \li \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/signalsandslots.html}{signals and slots}
        \li \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/model-view-programming.html}{Model/View architecture}
    \endlist

    While these concepts are exposed through a Rust-friendly API, familiarity
    with these Qt concepts will help you get the most out of building UIs with
    Qt Quick.

    \section1 Future Plans

    \list
        \li Enable interoperability with
        \l
        {https://crates.io/crates/cxx-qt}(https://crates.io/crates/cxx-qt){CXX-Qt}.
        \li Enable interoperability with other libraries, such as
        \l {https://tokio.rs/}{tokio}.
        \li Enable QObjects as properties (complex properties).
        \li Provide Qt dependencies as a crate to enable a streamlined
        installation through cargo.
        \li Remove the dependency on Qt binaries like qmake and maybe a C++ tool
        chain.
        \li Extend IDE support, in particular for VS Code, and enable the QML
        language server to understand types generated in Rust.
    \endlist

